WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.848
IEC 62443 distributes cybersecurity responsibility across the industrial lifecycle.

00:00:03.848 --> 00:00:10.512
The asset owner owns or operates the industrial environment and remains accountable for understanding operational risk,

00:00:10.512 --> 00:00:15.094
defining security requirements, approving residual risk, and sustaining the security program.

00:00:15.094 --> 00:00:20.092
System integrators and automation solution providers turn requirements into a working architecture.

00:00:20.092 --> 00:00:23.840
They select and configure components, define zones and conduits,

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.005
establish accounts and communication paths, test controls, and deliver documentation.

00:00:28.005 --> 00:00:32.170
Their work must satisfy both cybersecurity objectives and process requirements.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:36.335
Product suppliers develop hardware and software used in industrial systems.

00:00:36.335 --> 00:00:40.084
They are responsible for secure development practices, documented capabilities,

00:00:40.084 --> 00:00:41.750
vulnerability handling, update information,

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:45.915
and clear assumptions about how a product should be deployed.

00:00:45.915 --> 00:00:51.329
A product's capability is useful only when the system design uses it correctly.

00:00:51.329 --> 00:00:55.911
Service and maintenance providers preserve or modify the system after commissioning.

00:00:55.911 --> 00:00:58.826
Their remote access, tools, credentials, change practices,

00:00:58.826 --> 00:01:02.575
and incident communication become part of the security architecture.

00:01:02.575 --> 00:01:08.406
Contracts and operating procedures should define what access is permitted, how it is approved,

00:01:08.406 --> 00:01:10.905
and what evidence must be retained.

00:01:10.905 --> 00:01:13.820
Shared responsibility does not mean vague responsibility.

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:17.985
Projects should assign ownership for risk decisions, configuration, accounts, patching,

00:01:17.985 --> 00:01:21.317
backups, monitoring, incident response, documentation, and end-of-life planning.

00:01:21.317 --> 00:01:25.482
Handoffs between suppliers, integrators, and operators are common failure points.

00:01:25.482 --> 00:01:30.480
Acceptance criteria should verify that required controls work and that the asset

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:33.812
owner receives the information needed to operate them.

00:01:33.812 --> 00:01:39.227
Security is strongest when every role understands both its obligations and its dependencies.

00:01:39.227 --> 00:01:42.975
No participant can transfer all risk to another party,

00:01:42.975 --> 00:01:47.140
and no single participant can manage the entire lifecycle alone.
